body:
This topic was discussed in the Node.js loaders group meeting today (https://github.com/nodejs/loaders/issues/64), and a summary is [available in the notes](https://docs.google.com/document/d/16UqAIdLfOP8HR9cgHcSKk0NlkRfSzzXGCma0O3ly8Lw/edit).

The discussion found tentative support for a sync hook to avoid divergence, conditional on there being no architecture issues or strong user feedback against it. While there are some architecture complexities given the existing async userland loaders, this should be manageable on the current loader roadmap of moving loaders into their own threads so that their calls could be syncified.

The work to implement a sync resolver and `import.meta.resolve` function will take some time, and while it isn't possible to guarantee the path forward, there are currently no blocking concerns or use cases that have come up.

@annevk the suggestion of a JS hosts coordination space sounds ideal, and something much needed for `import.meta` host hook coordination amongst others, please sign me up as well.

> I was under the impression that [Stability: 1 - Experimental](https://nodejs.org/dist/latest-v17.x/docs/api/documentation.html#stability-index): "The feature is not subject to Semantic Versioning rules. Non-backward compatible changes or removal may occur in any future release.

@domenic this is exactly right - it's an experimental API and breaking changes are not only possible but expected. Let me be very clear - at no point was the goal to claim stability of the existing usage as a point of leverage in discussion. This PR came after the Node.js experimental `import.meta.resolve` feature was shipped. Within Node.js we have been experimentally implementing what was architecturally easiest and made sense for Node.js, while putting a lot of thought to cross-platform compatibility and while also thinking of future use cases. The question has only ever been about exploring the best options for Node.js and cross-platform workflows. I hope you'll agree that having room to discuss use cases, experiment and discuss alternative paths, and ensure engagement between stakeholders, like we have done here, is a critical part of that process.

@annevk ... a coordination group would be *fantastic*. Let's definitely do that.

@domenic ...
> ...But a lot of the rhetoric above indicates that Node.js is not only unwilling to change, but they want to prevent browsers from shipping an API with the same name, which is exactly the opposite of the kind of experimentation we want at this stage in discussions.

That's certainly not the impression that anyone in this thread has intended to give. The intent was solely: Let's not diverge APIs when it's not obviously necessary to do so, and in this instance it isn't obvious that we needed to. I certainly hope @guybedford's update from the loaders team is sufficient at putting your concern here at ease. That said, I still don't think the case has been made about why the API can't return a `Promise` but if the Node.js loaders folks think they can make it work then ok, I'll drop that issue.

It's safe to say that I think none of us on the Node.js side are here -- in any way, by any stretch of the imagination -- to try to force APIs to be one way or the other. We would just like to make sure that non-Web browser use cases are reasonably considered when the APIs in question impact more than just Web browsers, and that we can avoid diverging APIs whenever possible.

@domenic:
> On that collaborative note, I want to try to clarify some of what is upsetting to me about how this discussion has gone. Unilaterally shipping an API, using a name which was already proposed in a standards body for use with different semantics, is not how we prefer to work in the WHATWG.

To clarify one key point here... This is absolutely *not* what has happened here. The PR that added the experimental implementation of `import.meta.resolve()` in Node.js was [opened Dec 19th 2019 and landed on Feb 3, 2020](https://github.com/nodejs/node/pull/31032) and was shipped in a release a few weeks later.  Well in advance of this PR being opened. The `import.meta.resolve()` polyfill/shim in `es-module-shims` [was landed](https://github.com/guybedford/es-module-shims/commit/34f1d5cbf0f7b2e0182bdf8258c58a4b534da670) Aug 7, 2020. (and yes, I'm aware of the earlier conversation that was happening in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3871)

@GeoffreyBooth:
> ...So proposing a change for the resolve loader hook to become sync would also need to involve discussions with those teams, to confirm that alternate solutions could be found for their tools to do what they need to do while resolution overall is sync.

Given that those discussions are likely going to take some time to... ahem... resolve, and given the likelihood that those additional edge cases are likely to be specific to Node.js (and similar runtimes), should an async resolve be necessary, then would the loaders team be ok with introducing a differently named method than `import.meta.resolve()` that can be implemented adjacent to the blocking `import.meta.resolve()` defined here, or is the expectation such that we would need a single resolve implementation?
